<Project Sdk="Microsoft.NET.Sdk">

  <!--
    Hris.Modules.Timekeeping
    Phase 3 (Workforce Management), Sprint 3, per IMPLEMENTATION-PLAN.md
    ("This ordering is easy to get backwards... Attendance records are evaluated
    against those rules. Building attendance first means having nothing to evaluate
    against.").

    Source specification: docs/04-modules/timekeeping/.

    This module owns the rules that define expected work, and deliberately owns
    nothing about what actually happened on any given day. Clock events, attendance
    records, corrections, tardiness, undertime, overtime amount, and absence
    detection all belong to the attendance module built in Sprint 4. Timekeeping's
    only contribution to overtime is the OvertimeEligible flag on WorkShift, which
    is a property of the shift definition rather than of any day worked.

    Four Aggregate Roots per domain/aggregates.md: WorkSchedule (the recurring
    working-day pattern, holding its coarse organizational assignments as child
    entities), WorkShift (the concrete daily working-time definition), ShiftAssignment
    (one employee-or-unit-to-shift binding, its own root), and HolidayCalendar (a
    dated, layered, versioned holiday set holding Holiday child entities).

    The two assignment-shaped decisions point in opposite directions on purpose, and
    that document says so explicitly. Schedule assignment is coarse, infrequent, and
    reviewed together with the schedule it adopts, so it lives inside WorkSchedule.
    Shift assignment is high-churn, independently swapped, and a swap involves two
    employees at once, so it stands alone with its own repository. Neither is an
    oversight.

    The module's core property is determinism: the same query, for the same employee
    and the same past date, must always return the same shift and the same holiday
    determination regardless of when it is asked. Payroll two phases downstream
    computes pay from what this module says was true, and a rule that answers
    differently on Tuesday than it did on Monday produces payroll that is internally
    consistent, plausible, and wrong. TK-001 through TK-003 are that property
    expressed as invariants: superseded versions are never edited, resolution selects
    the version in force on the date being evaluated rather than the version current
    at query time, and publishing a revision never triggers a retroactive rewrite of
    already-finalized downstream results.

    This module's own root namespace segment is "Timekeeping", and none of its four
    Aggregate Roots is named Timekeeping, so the CS0118 name-resolution collision
    Organization, Position, Employment, and Employee each hit does not recur here.

    Deliberately does NOT reference Hris.Foundation.Scheduling. That framework is
    general-purpose cron and job execution which happens to share the word
    "schedule"; README.md's own boundary table states this module never calls into it
    and is never a consumer of it.
  -->

  <PropertyGroup>
    <RootNamespace>Hris.Modules.Timekeeping</RootNamespace>
  </PropertyGroup>

  <ItemGroup>
    <PackageReference Include="MediatR" Version="12.4.1" />
    <PackageReference Include="FluentValidation" Version="11.11.0" />
    <PackageReference Include="FluentValidation.DependencyInjectionExtensions" Version="11.11.0" />
    <PackageReference Include="Microsoft.EntityFrameworkCore" Version="9.0.4" />
    <PackageReference Include="Microsoft.Extensions.DependencyInjection.Abstractions" Version="9.0.4" />
  </ItemGroup>

  <ItemGroup>
    <ProjectReference Include="..\..\BuildingBlocks\Hris.SharedKernel\Hris.SharedKernel.csproj" />
    <ProjectReference Include="..\..\BuildingBlocks\Hris.Application\Hris.Application.csproj" />
    <ProjectReference Include="..\..\BuildingBlocks\Hris.Infrastructure\Hris.Infrastructure.csproj" />
  </ItemGroup>

</Project>
